# Target C-II — genuine two-swap TB1 blockers collapse to the `D^-2` boundary shells

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** parity-thinned one-sign strong-TB1 exact-swap family through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** active-diameter decoupling; common oriented TB1 swap-axis cone; uniform `q_i=O(D^-1)` exact-swap scale; superdiameter-thrackle lemma; repeated-witness collapse  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_binary_obstruction_inheritance_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The active-diameter decoupling theorem reduces the parity-thinned one-sign strong-TB1 branch to a Boolean finite-swap system. Every retained layer `i` has an exact contact-swap vector

\[
v_i=-q_iw_i,
\qquad
q_i=O(D^{-1}),
\]

and all oriented axes satisfy

\[
|w_i-w_j|=O(D^{-1}).
\]

Any previously strict pair depends on at most the two swap coordinates containing its endpoints. The remaining question was whether genuinely binary interaction between two swaps could create a new large inactive obstruction system unrelated to the already-known singleton witnesses.

It cannot, except inside the `O(D^-2)` boundary shells.

The new local statement is:

> **Max-swap inheritance lemma.** Consider a previously strict pair with endpoints in two retained moved layers `i,j`. Suppose `q_i>=q_j`. If the pair is safe both initially and under the singleton exact swap at `i`, but violates the unit or diameter bound when **both** swaps `i,j` are applied, then
> \[
> |xy|-1=O(D^{-2})
> \]
> in the lower-bound case, or
> \[
> D-|xy|=O(D^{-2})
> \]
> in the upper-bound case.

Thus, outside the two ultra-thin shells, a violation under the joint state `11` is always inherited from the singleton move of the endpoint with the larger swap magnitude `q`.

Consequently every genuinely new two-coordinate obstruction of the simultaneous exact-swap family is either

\[
1<|xy|\le1+O(D^{-2}),
\]

or

\[
D-O(D^{-2})\le|xy|<D.
\]

The second class is automatically above the sharp threshold `sqrt(D^2-1)` for all sufficiently large `D`, so every such upper interaction blocker belongs to the geometric superdiameter thrackle.

This also gives a useful Boolean normal form. Away from the `D^-2` shells, any pair that blocks a singleton move and remains blocking after its partner layer is also swapped must be oriented from the **larger** swap magnitude to the smaller one. Hence all non-shell persistent binary blockers strictly decrease `q`; they cannot form a directed cycle. A `11`-only blocker, or a blocker inherited from the smaller-`q` endpoint while the larger-`q` singleton is safe, is necessarily ultra-thin.

The strong-TB1 finite-swap frontier is therefore narrower than a generic arity-two constraint system: all genuinely binary synergy is confined to the same two rigid shells already isolated elsewhere in the project.

---

## 1. Two-coordinate displacement geometry

Take two retained nonadjacent one-sign layers `i,j`. Let

\[
v_i=-q_iw_i,
\qquad
v_j=-q_jw_j,
\tag{BI.1}
\]

where, uniformly over the retained family,

\[
0<q_i,q_j\le {Q\over D}
\tag{BI.2}
\]

and

\[
|w_i-w_j|\le {C\over D}
\tag{BI.3}
\]

for fixed constants `Q,C` and all sufficiently large sources. The existing exact-swap theorem supplies such fixed constants.

Orient a previously strict pair `xy` so that its endpoint in `R_i` contributes `v_i` and its endpoint in `R_j` contributes `-v_j` to the relative vector. Write

\[
r=y-x,
\qquad
1<|r|<D.
\tag{BI.4}
\]

The four Boolean states are

\[
r_{00}=r,
\quad
r_{10}=r-q_iw_i,
\quad
r_{01}=r+q_jw_j,
\quad
r_{11}=r-q_iw_i+q_jw_j.
\tag{BI.5}
\]

Assume first

\[
q_i\ge q_j.
\tag{BI.6}
\]

Put

\[
s={q_j\over q_i}\in(0,1],
\qquad
t=1-s.
\tag{BI.7}
\]

Then

\[
-q_iw_i+q_jw_j
=t(-q_iw_i)+e,
\tag{BI.8}
\]

where

\[
e=q_j(w_j-w_i)
\tag{BI.9}
\]

and therefore

\[
|e|
\le {Cq_j\over D}
={Csq_i\over D}.
\tag{BI.10}
\]

Define the point on the segment from the `00` state to the larger-`q` singleton state

\[
z=r-tq_iw_i
=s r+t(r-q_iw_i).
\tag{BI.11}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{r_{11}=z+e.}
\tag{BI.12}
\]

So the joint displacement is an exact convex interpolation between `00` and the larger singleton `10`, plus only an `O(D^-2)` transverse error.

The exact squared-norm interpolation identity gives

\[
\boxed{
|z|^2
=s|r|^2+t|r-q_iw_i|^2-stq_i^2.
}
\tag{BI.13}
\]

This identity is the entire mechanism behind the shell collapse.

---

## 2. Lower interaction blocker implies `1+O(D^-2)`

Assume the original pair and the larger-`q` singleton state both satisfy the strict lower bound,

\[
|r|>1,
\qquad
|r-q_iw_i|>1,
\tag{BI.14}
\]

but the joint state violates it:

\[
|r_{11}|=|z+e|\le1.
\tag{BI.15}
\]

Equation (BI.15) gives

\[
|z|\le1+|e|,
\]

hence

\[
|z|^2-1\le2|e|+|e|^2.
\tag{BI.16}
\]

Subtracting one from (BI.13), using the positivity of both endpoint lower slacks, and discarding the second positive weighted slack gives

\[
s(|r|^2-1)
\le
2|e|+|e|^2+stq_i^2.
\tag{BI.17}
\]

Use (BI.10) and divide by `s>0`:

\[
|r|^2-1
\le
{2Cq_i\over D}
+{sC^2q_i^2\over D^2}
+tq_i^2.
\tag{BI.18}
\]

With `q_i<=Q/D`,

\[
\boxed{
|r|^2-1
\le
{2CQ+Q^2\over D^2}
+{Q^2C^2\over D^4}.
}
\tag{BI.19}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{|r|-1=O(D^{-2}).}
\tag{BI.20}
\]

No lower bound on either swap size is used. If `q_j/q_i` is tiny, the error term in (BI.10) shrinks by the same factor that is divided out in (BI.17).

---

## 3. Upper interaction blocker implies `D-O(D^-2)`

Now assume the original pair and the larger-`q` singleton state both satisfy the strict diameter upper bound,

\[
|r|<D,
\qquad
|r-q_iw_i|<D,
\tag{BI.21}
\]

but

\[
|r_{11}|=|z+e|\ge D.
\tag{BI.22}
\]

Because `z` is a convex combination of two points in the open radius-`D` disk,

\[
|z|<D.
\tag{BI.23}
\]

The joint violation yields

\[
D^2-|z|^2
\le
|z+e|^2-|z|^2
\le
2D|e|+|e|^2.
\tag{BI.24}
\]

From (BI.13),

\[
D^2-|z|^2
=s(D^2-|r|^2)
+t(D^2-|r-q_iw_i|^2)
+stq_i^2.
\tag{BI.25}
\]

All terms on the right are positive. Hence

\[
s(D^2-|r|^2)
\le2D|e|+|e|^2.
\tag{BI.26}
\]

Using (BI.10) and dividing by `s`,

\[
D^2-|r|^2
\le
2Cq_i+{sC^2q_i^2\over D^2}
\le
{2CQ\over D}+{Q^2C^2\over D^4}.
\tag{BI.27}
\]

The joint upper violation and `|r_{11}-r|=O(D^-1)` already imply `|r|=D-O(D^-1)`, so `D+|r|>=D` for all sufficiently large sources. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
D-|r|
={D^2-|r|^2\over D+|r|}
=O(D^{-2}).
}
\tag{BI.28}
\]

This is one full power of `D` thinner than the original exact-swap inactive window.

---

## 4. Symmetry and the max-swap inheritance law

If instead

\[
q_j\ge q_i,
\]

the same argument is applied with the `01` singleton state as the larger move. Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{l}
\text{if `11` violates a boundary while `00` and the singleton state of}\
\text{the larger-`q` endpoint are safe, then the original pair lies}\
\text{within }O(D^{-2})\text{ of that boundary.}
\end{array}}
\tag{BI.29}
\]

Equivalently, outside the two shells

\[
1<|r|\le1+O(D^{-2})
\tag{BI.30}
\]

and

\[
D-O(D^{-2})\le|r|<D,
\tag{BI.31}
\]

we have the implication

\[
\boxed{
11\text{ bad}
\Longrightarrow
\text{the larger-}q\text{ singleton is already bad.}
}
\tag{BI.32}
\]

This is stronger than saying that every pair has support on at most two swap coordinates. It says that non-shell two-coordinate coupling has no new `11` obstruction mode.

---

## 5. Global subset consequence

Take any nonempty subset `S` of the parity-thinned exact-swap coordinates. Old contacts and old actual diameters are safe by the active-diameter decoupling theorem.

Suppose a previously strict pair obstructs the move on `S`.

- If only one endpoint of the pair lies in a selected moved layer, its state under `S` is exactly one singleton state. The obstruction is already a singleton obstruction.
- If both endpoints lie in selected moved layers, order them so the larger exact-swap magnitude is `q_i>=q_j`. If the same pair also blocks singleton `i`, the obstruction is inherited from that singleton. If singleton `i` is safe, (BI.29) forces the pair into one of the `D^-2` shells.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{every non-shell obstruction of every subset move is inherited from}\
\text{a singleton exact-swap obstruction at its larger-}q\text{ endpoint.}
}
\tag{BI.33}
\]

So all **genuine binary synergy** in the inactive system lies in the ultra-thin shells.

---

## 6. Upper synergy is automatically a geometric thrackle

If an upper interaction blocker satisfies

\[
D-|r|\le {K\over D^2}
\tag{BI.34}
\]

for one fixed `K`, then for all sufficiently large `D`,

\[
D-{K\over D^2}
>
D-{1\over2D}
>
\sqrt{D^2-1}.
\tag{BI.35}
\]

Hence every upper-shell genuine binary blocker satisfies

\[
\boxed{|r|>\sqrt{D^2-1}.}
\tag{BI.36}
\]

By the superdiameter-thrackle lemma, the entire upper interaction family is a straight-line geometric thrackle: any two such chords share an endpoint or cross properly, and all their endpoints lie on the convex hull.

Thus the only genuinely binary upper obstruction is not merely an inactive near-diameter pair; it already belongs to the same sharp crossing-order class isolated in the SUPERTHIN branch.

---

## 7. Boolean pattern normal form outside the shells

Write the three nonzero local states of a pair as `10`, `01`, `11`.

The max-swap inheritance law and the earlier repeated-witness collapse imply the following outside the `O(D^-2)` shells.

1. A `11`-only blocker is impossible.
2. A pair cannot block both singleton states `10` and `01`; such a repeated blocker is ultra-thin by the previous double-witness theorem.
3. If `10` is bad, `01` is safe, and `11` is also bad, then necessarily
   \[
   \boxed{q_i>q_j.}
   \tag{BI.37}
   \]
   Otherwise `q_j>=q_i`, so the larger-`q` singleton `01` would be safe while `11` is bad, forcing the shell by (BI.29).
4. Symmetrically, `01` and `11` bad with `10` safe forces `q_j>q_i`.

Hence every non-shell pair constraint has one of two nontrivial forms:

```text
DISARMABLE IMPLICATION:
    one singleton is bad,
    but selecting the partner makes 11 safe;

Q-DESCENDING PERSISTENT BLOCKER:
    one singleton is bad and 11 remains bad,
    with q_source > q_partner.
```

The persistent-blocker digraph is therefore acyclic because `q` strictly decreases along every edge.

All departures from this implication / descending-obstruction normal form are forced into

```text
1 + O(D^-2) lower shell
or
D - O(D^-2) upper shell.
```

The upper shell is a geometric thrackle by Section 6.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The audit before this note reduced the one-sign strong-TB1 branch to a linear family of nonadjacent exact finite swaps whose old active constraints completely decouple. The apparent remaining object was a generic Boolean arity-two inactive constraint system.

It is not generic.

After this note, the valid finite-swap obstruction system has the form

```text
linear parity-thinned exact-swap coordinates
+ every old active constraint safe for every subset

inactive blockers:
    non-shell part
        -> inherited from singleton blockers
        -> disarmable implications, or
        -> persistent edges strictly decreasing q

    genuine binary part
        -> 1 + O(D^-2) ultra-near-unit pairs
        or
        -> D - O(D^-2) superdiameter thrackle chords.
```

This does not yet produce a nonzero safe subset. A directed implication system can still funnel into `q`-descending persistent blockers, and the lower ultra-near-unit shell is not yet ruled out. But the remaining closure target is now sharper: one must eliminate or exploit the `D^-2` shell families, or show that the implication / descending-`q` normal form cannot block all nonzero exact-swap subsets in the actual path-layer geometry.

No claim of C-II closure is made.
